a</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Nicoletti</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Angelini</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Infection with chikungunya virus in Italy: an outbreak in a temperate region</article-title><source>Lancet</source><year>2007</year><volume>370</volume><fpage>1840</fpage><lpage>1846</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">18061059</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R70"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Asnis</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name><name><surname>Conetta</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Waldman</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><article-title>Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Outbreak of West Nile-like viral encephalitis &#x02014; New York, 1999</article-title><source>MMWR</source><year>1999</year><volume>48</volume><fpage>845</fpage><lpage>849</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10563521</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Figure</label><caption><p>A composite representation of the ancient eastern Nile Delta (not to scale) that indicates the proposed general locations of biblical sites and a diagram (to scale) of the present day region [derived from information in <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R8">8</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R9">9</xref>,<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="R10">10</xref>]. As a port city, Rameses would have had direct access to one of the ancient branches of the Nile River shown north of modern Cairo.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="yjbm_81_1_31_g01"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>